To me this is one of the joy's of Nethack.  The DevTeam
set the prices of items for reasons.  What *is* the
reason in any one case?  If an object has a high price
and I don't understand why it means there are ideas I
have missed.

Some prices make sense to early folks, some make sense
to folks exploring the Quest, Castle and going into
Gehenom the first few times, some make sense only after
extended play.  For that matter some make sense early
on and then the sense seems to taper off.  Why bother
with the ultra-high price of a dilithium crystal when
there's no longer a shopkeeper in the dungeon that
won't just die at a few hits of your super-weapon
attacks?  Some high prices seem to be there to trick
mid-game folks into valuing them as much as anything
else.  Other prices are high for a very good reason that
can take a while to figure out.

Take weapon prices for example.  It took me a long time
to figure out why bows cost so much in stores.  Yet
once I learned the value of missiles I've started to
wonder why daggers aren't the highest priced weapons.


